\f1\fs22 A mostly empty bar, a low-lit bunker lined with accrued stains and the taste of every argument ever lost under a flickering smart-poster of the Ulyssian flag. Halo nursed a glass of something regional and disagreeable at his elbow, barely registering the sticky lacquer of the table. His attention fixed on the trisected holoscreens over the bar, each blaring multi-angle feeds of a siege in progress somewhere up in the Ghetto.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The broadcast\'92s anchor barely concealed his excitement: \'93In the early hours of the morning," he said, squinting at the camera like it owed him money, "police surrounded this run-down mansion in the Ghetto district of the Pale.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo sipped, lips twitching.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The journo continued. \'93Channel Forty-Eight has since learned the operation may be led by the celebrated Public Affairs Bureau.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Taxing Shakti!\'94 Halo swore. \'93Don\'92t you tax this up!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93In a Channel Forty-Eight exclusive, we can reveal that we suspect\'97or hope to suspect\'97this is the operational nerve centre of the not-for-profit terrorist Anthony Chantara.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Stay out of it, you excuse of a marsupial!\'94 Halo cursed under his breath. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera swung to several Elvi in paramilitary ponchos manning an ornate gate in a tall wall. \'93As you can see, the grounds are heavily guarded by Elvi, and defended by tanks, which Channel Forty-Eight exclusively understands are the same tanks used to break not-for-profit terrorist Chantara out of a maximum-security facility a little over a month ago.\'94 As the bars of the gate blurred, the camera panned across five or six tanks squatting like gigantic sleeping cane toads on the lawn between the gate and a battered mansion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now, Chantara is wanted for many crimes, including Black Friday felonies against the Floormart department store chain, the bombing of the Stickett Inn, and the Scarlett Shower Surgeon killings.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo sucked his drink through clenched teeth as the broadcast cut to an archival segment of Chanta: battered, bruised and breathless, standing near the burning, jagged shell of a Floormart. \'93People are dying in there!\'94 Chanta blurted, angry and desperate. \'93They just backed the trucks up and dumped a whole load of gymnasium equipment on Girl Guides selling Anzac cookies. I nearly got clobbered... I only went in to buy new underwear.\'94 Chanta, rubbing soot from his eyes with a shaking hand, held up a packet of boxer shorts, tears streaking down his cheeks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Video cut back to the journo. \'93There you have it,\'94 he said. \'93What kind of monster would buy pink underwear with fairy motifs, when he could have prevented a charitable department store from forcing innocent children into slave labour?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Suddenly, several off-camera voices shouted, \'93He\'92s here! He\'92s here!\'94 The journo gasped and dashed off: \'93Lieutenant Shakti! Lieutenant Shakti!\'94 He hurried after a man in a macintosh whose posture suggested he\'92d rather be climbing gum trees than indulging press.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We love 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 !\'94 Mudrake called, huffing to catch up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander halted and turned abruptly; the camera operator nearly collided with him. By the time the shot was re-framed, Alexander was directing a Kubrick stare over wild eyebrows, his eyes koala-beady, his mouth curling in a way he believed looked both sardonic and humorous. \'93Of course, my good fellow.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Can you tell us what\'92s happening here? It seems the Public Affairs Bureau has surrounded this property. Can we hope for a breakthrough? Is Chantara holed up here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, Drake\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s Blake. Blake Mudrake, Channel Forty-Eight Sty News.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander frowned, jaw tensed, gaze drifting off-camera. \'93Well, Blake, we\'92re on location filming scenes for a Candid Criminal special\'85\'94 Alexander paused, expecting applause.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, how exciting! A special episode! You heard it here first, folks\'97a Channel Forty-Eight exclusive.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander half-smiled, half-grimaced in impatience.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Blake pressed. \'93Are we about to witness the re-arrest of not-for-profit terrorist Anthony Chantara?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ll just have to wait for the broadcast. But I can say we\'92ll be delving into issues deeper than domestic terrorism.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Will the mystery of Airborne Sarah Jacobson\'92s abduction be revealed? Is she here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Again, I can\'92t pre-empt the show, but we know Sarah is in good health, showered in gifts and love since this all started."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what of the deeper issues?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander almost bridled at that. \'93The corrupt state of domestic security in Ulyssia. The Ulyssian intelligence agencies\'97in particular, a clandestine group called PAPER, who you may never have heard of\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo, watching on, crushed a handful of peanuts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ve heard of them,\'94 Mudrake said. \'93Everyone has.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander ignored him. \'93These agencies have been stepping in on roles that rightfully belong to TRAMPS. Their secrecy erodes standards, ethics, and inevitably, competence. For example, it was PAPER\'92s incompetence that allowed Chantara to escape during the filming of 
\f2\i Curb Your Individualism
\f1\i0 . On our 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0  special expos\'e9, we\'92ll show why that show should be cancelled and its production team charged with crimes against entertainment. We have to stop these faceless PAPER\'85 faces. TRAMPS have a face. It\'92s my face. Look at my face\'97this is a face you can trust."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m confused, Lieutenant Shakti. If PAPER is so incompetent, and 
\f2\i C.Y.I.
\f1\i0  is so untrustworthy\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It is.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And you were the one who arrested Chantara in the first place\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I was.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then could you explain how Chantara ended up on 
\f2\i C.Y.I.
\f1\i0  rather than 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 ?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander froze, broke character, and slumped, throwing up his hands as he glared off camera at a producer. \'93Has this idiot even read the script?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> Haloren Smythe watched the broadcast go momentarily black until it recommenced with a pair of news anchors at a desk in a studio.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Blake Mudrake there with that exclusive report,\'94 said one through an anchor-person smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Exciting news about a 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0  special expos\'e9
\f2\i , 
\f1\i0 isn\'92t it?\'94 effused the other.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, yes! I can hardly wait. I makes me wonder if any PAPER agents get trebuchet slaps,\'94 enjoined the first, the studio light sparkle in her eyes synchronising with the frozen fixation of her smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That would be slamming wouldn\'92t it?\'94 asked the second. \'93Is that your favourite option on the Wheel of Death, Stacy?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There are so many good options, Macy, but I don\'92t think you can go past the humour of the helium chamber. Call me old-fashioned; I can\'92t stop giggling about their funny high-pitched voices.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s a good one for sure, Stacy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What\'92s your favourite, Macy?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, just hang \'91em, I say.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Stacy nodded.\'94\'91Mmmm\'85. traditional\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Anyway, the weather now, and you Cloudborne can look forward to a block of thick Layer cloud settle even lower over the harbour until offshore winds bring more of the Layer in the late afternoon and early evening.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo had stopped paying attention. He was still grinding his teeth well the broadcast cut from the goofy face of Alexander Shakti back to the studio. But it wasn\'92t Shakti haunting the inside of Halo\'92s skull\'97no, it was that dull, metronomic thwack-thwack-thwack buzzing away in the background of the live cross. While Shakti droned, Halo let his eyes roam to the sky boxed into the screens\'92 upper corners, expecting a flock of PAPER gunships to descend out of the greenish-grey wool of the Layer and scramble his covert op to breakfast.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As a brace of polite, implacably ambitious production assistants shooed the news crew away, Alexander\'92s phone rang.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hello, Mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I just saw you on the news,\'94 Eloise said, voice straining to contain her animus.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, you did?\'94 Alexander squeezed his knees together in glee. \'93Are you proud of me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alexander, I am as proud of you now as I was in all your other attempts at something so inanely ambitious.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Really, Mother? You think it\'92s ambitious?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise closed her eyes. She seldom used modifiers, especially adverbs, but when she did, she expected they\'92d be noted. Still, she lacked the heart to crush him and doubled down: \'93Actually, I believe this might be the most inanely ambitious caper you\'92ve bungled yet.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, thank you, Mother. Coming from you, that means the world.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise had to admit, this was about as close to a compliment as her son could expect from her. \'93Alexander, dear, might I offer a few notes? I\'92m concerned about\'97\'94 she considered how best to phrase it \'93\'97a few plot holes.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Plot holes?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise shaped every word with care. \'93Perhaps I\'92m missing something here. Can you walk me through the arc of this story? What\'92s the protagonist\'92s objective?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, Mother, isn\'92t it obvious?\'94 Alexander larded his reply with condescension\'97Eloise swallowed it as gracefully as she could and resigned herself to letting him mansplain. \'93I\'92m arresting Chantara\'97again\'97and this time I won\'92t let PAPER tax it all up. And\'85\'94 Alexander paused, both for effect and to conceal his real motive. \'93\'85I\'92m rescuing Sarah.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alexander, we\'92ve discussed this. There\'92s no need for a man of your\'85charms\'85to tether himself to one woman.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But I love her, Mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I know, I know.\'94 He loves her about as much as he loved that blue-tongue lizard he kept as a boy. And, as with all things, he tired of Flynn Lizzy soon enough, decided to dissect him, and when you realised he couldn\'92t put him back together he sawed off head and tail alike. \'93Remind me, how do you plan to get past the tanks?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, I was going to call you about that, Mother. Could you disable them while I go in?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise paused. \'93Alexander, you can\'92t just stroll Chantara or Sarah out of there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course I can. I have the place surrounded.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m more concerned with who has you surrounded.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What are you talking about?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Darling, do you hear that thrumming in the background?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now you mention it, yes. Can you hear it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not personally, no.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What of it, Mother? Just some bloke mowing his lawn, I guess.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mowing his lawn. In the Ghetto.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sure, why not?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, anything\'92s possible if you\'92re mowing our lawn with over a dozen PAPER gunships.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What are you talking about?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re being monitored. You\'92re always being monitored. And whenever you do something inanely ambitious\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You really do think it\'92s ambitious, Mother?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'97you attract a great deal of attention.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander quite enjoyed attracting attention.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise pressed on. \'93What do you expect PAPER will do when those tanks are disabled?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have our own gunships, Mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You have police cruisers with guns, for quelling protests. They have military hardware\'97meant for real trouble. I ought to know, I sold it to them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So you can disable their weapons, too.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She had to admit, there was a perverse logic. Yet Alexander exhausted her, always. \'93Only if I fancy exile,\'94 she said. \'93Or a coup.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92d be amazing, Mother. You\'92d make a cracker CEO.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92d be the most bored CEO alive,\'94 Eloise replied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You could always make me CEO.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Absolutely, Alexander. Check your stocking come Christmas.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander fell into awed silence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Look,\'94 Eloise said. \'93If I neutralise the tanks, PAPER wins the field. In truth, they already have the advantage,  but they\'92ll wait until you show your hand. If they twig that Chantara and Sarah are inside, they\'92ll have all the reason in the world to come in, guns blazing. I doubt they\'92ll shed a tear over a little collateral damage.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But Mother, I can\'92t leave Sarah there a moment longer.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just stall until I arrive. Don\'92t do anything rash.\'94 Eloise caught herself. \'93Actually, don\'92t do anything at all.\'94
